**Describe the bug**
In the [documentation](https://solang.readthedocs.io/en/latest/language/statements.html#try-catch-statement), it is stated that 

> There is an alternate syntax which avoids the abi decoding by leaving the catch Error(…) out. This might be useful when no error string is expected ...

How-ever, I found an interesting edge case which does not line up with what the documentation says.

As a side note this documentation page needs some overhaul; the examples are not (no longer?) working properly, I can take care of that.

**To Reproduce**
Consider the following contracts, adapted closely from the documentation mentioned above:

```solidity
contract aborting {
    constructor(bool abort) {
        if (abort) {
            revert("bar");
        }
    }

    function foo() public pure {}
}

contract runner {
    function test() public {
        try new aborting(true) returns (aborting a) {
            // new succeeded; a holds the a reference to the new contract
        } catch Error(string x) {
            if (x == "bar") {
                // "bar" revert or require was executed
            }
        } catch (bytes raw) {
            // if no error string could decoding, we end up here with the raw data
        }
    }
}
```

If the `runner` contract is instantiate without any funds, the underlying `seal_instantiate` call fails (with error code 5 / transfer failed). How-ever, based on what's written in the documentation I'd expect this to be catched inside the `catch (bytes raw)` block. Instead, the contract traps.

For this case, the `seal_instantiate` error _can_ actually be catched and handled, so that the runner contract does _not_ trap, like so:

```solidity
contract aborting {
    constructor(bool abort) {
        if (abort) {
            revert("bar");
        }
    }

    function foo() public pure {}
}

contract runner {
    function test() public {
        try new aborting(true) returns (aborting a) {
            // new succeeded; a holds the a reference to the new contract
        } catch {
            // NOTE: We end actually up here if seal_instantiate runs out of funds
        }
    }
}
```

But this is not documented that way.

**Expected behavior**
I think that either the documentation should be updated to point this out, if this behavior is expected and correct (CC @seanyoung please call me out if I'm wrong here). Or, we make sure the contract does not trap when there is nothing written into the output buffer.

